# ACT ONE

= Mara, a ferry pilot, is told her crossing is being shut down.

## The harbour

EXT. GULL ISLAND HARBOUR - DAWN #1#

Fog sits on the water. A small car ferry, the *Persistence*, bumps against the pier.

MARA OKAFOR (50s, oilskin coat, a thermos older than most of the crew) walks the gangway, counting rivets out of habit.

MARA
Forty-one. Still forty-one.

TEO (O.S.)
You say that every morning!

TEO PARK (20s), deckhand, jogs down the pier with a paper bag of rolls.

TEO
(out of breath)
They were out of the seeded ones.

MARA
Then we sail hungry.

TEO
We sail with *plain* rolls. It's not a tragedy.

[[Keep the rivet count; it pays off in the last scene.]]

INT. PERSISTENCE - WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS #2#

Brass instruments, a cracked radio, a postcard of a mountain taped above the wheel.

Mara pours coffee. Teo leans in the doorway.

TEO
Did you read the letter?

MARA
Which letter?

TEO
The one you put under the thermos so you wouldn't have to read it.

Mara lifts the thermos. There is, in fact, a letter.

MARA (CONT'D)
I read the first line.
  
That was enough.

INT./EXT. PERSISTENCE - DECK AND WHEELHOUSE - MOMENTS LATER

Mara steps out with the letter. Teo follows.

MARA
"Owing to the completion of the bridge..."

TEO
What bridge?

MARA
Exactly.

CUT TO:

EST. THE NEW BRIDGE - DAY

A gleaming span reaches across the strait, unfinished at its middle. Two cranes nod at each other like old men.

I/E MARA'S TRUCK - DAY

Mara drives along the coast road, the bridge filling the windscreen.

MARA (V.O.)
Thirty years. Every crossing logged. Every storm.

She switches off the radio. The silence is louder.

.FLASHBACK - THE PERSISTENCE, THIRTY YEARS EARLIER

A YOUNGER MARA stands at the wheel beside OLD CAPTAIN VOSS, who smokes a pipe he never lights.

CAPTAIN VOSS
The sea doesn't care if you're brave. It cares if you're early.

YOUNGER MARA
(grinning)
Then I'll be early.

/*
INT. HARBOUR OFFICE - DAY

A cut scene. The harbour master explains the bridge budget.
Too much exposition; the letter does the job.

HARBOUR MASTER
Forty million, give or take.
*/

EXT/INT. HARBOUR CAFÉ - DAY #3A#

Through the window: Mara and Teo at a corner table. Inside: the clatter of plates.

TEO
We could run tours. Birdwatchers. Weddings.

MARA
Weddings.

TEO
People get married on boats!

MARA
People get *seasick* on boats.

They both reach for the last roll at the same time.

TEO
Mine.

MARA ^
Mine.

Neither lets go.

!THEY ARE BOTH TOO STUBBORN TO SPLIT IT.

The roll tears in half anyway.

> BURN TO WHITE.

===

# ACT TWO

= The storm arrives the night before the last crossing.

INT. MARA'S COTTAGE - NIGHT

Wind rattles the windows. Mara sits at the kitchen table with the logbook, turning pages.

	Entry after entry, in her own handwriting:
	"Calm. Seven cars. One goat."
	"Rough. Two cars. No goat."

The radio crackles.

RADIO (V.O.)
...gale warning for the strait, all vessels advised to remain in port...

A KNOCK at the door.

MARA
It's open!

Teo comes in, soaked.

TEO
The bridge crew can't get across. There's a woman on the far side. She's in labour.

Mara closes the logbook.

MARA
How far along?

TEO
Far enough that she's shouting at the nurse.

MARA
Then we're late.

CUT TO:

EXT. GULL ISLAND HARBOUR - NIGHT

Rain sideways. Mara and Teo cast off the lines.

~Row me over, row me home,
~Past the gull and past the foam,
~If the tide is turning slow,
~Row me where the lanterns glow.

TEO
(shouting over the wind)
Were you *singing*?

MARA
(shouting back)
Voss's song. Every crossing.

TEO
You've never sung it before!

MARA
You've never been on the night run before.

INT. PERSISTENCE - WHEELHOUSE - NIGHT

The instruments jump. A wave hits the bow; the postcard falls from the wall.

Mara catches it without looking.

TEO
How do you *do* that?

MARA
Thirty years.

@McKENZIE (V.O.)
(over radio, crackling)
Persistence, this is McKenzie on the bridge crew, do you read?

MARA
Reading you, McKenzie.

@McKENZIE (V.O.)
She's at the old slipway. You'll see our torches.

EXT. THE STRAIT - CONTINUOUS

The ferry climbs a wave and drops. Far ahead, small lights wave in the dark.

_Every light in the town is on._

INT. PERSISTENCE - CAR DECK - LATER

The ferry is tied up at the slipway. NADIA (30s) is helped aboard by McKENZIE (60s) and a NURSE.

NADIA
(between breaths)
Is this... the ferry... they're closing?

MARA
Not tonight it isn't.

NURSE
We won't make the hospital.

MARA
Then the hospital will have to come to us.

[[Should Teo faint here? Probably funnier if he doesn't.]]

FADE TO:

INT. PERSISTENCE - CAR DECK - LATER

A baby's cry over the engine noise.

Everyone exhales at once.

TEO
What's her name?

NADIA
(exhausted, smiling)
I was going to ask the captain.

Mara looks at the postcard in her hand.

MARA
Voss was a good name.

NADIA
For a girl?

MARA
For anyone who's early.

===

# ACT THREE

= The last scheduled crossing, and what comes after.

EXT. GULL ISLAND HARBOUR - DAY #9#

Sunshine. Bunting. The bridge stands finished behind the harbour.

A small crowd waits on the pier with cake. A banner reads: **THANK YOU PERSISTENCE**.

Mara walks the gangway, counting.

MARA
Forty-one. [[the rivets]] Still forty-one.

TEO
(handing her a roll)
Seeded.

MARA
Now *that* is a tragedy averted.

The crowd cheers as the ferry pulls away from the pier.

EXT. THE STRAIT - DAY

The *Persistence* passes under the new bridge. Drivers on the bridge honk and wave.

MARA (V.O.)
They say a bridge never closes. They're probably right.

Teo takes the wheel. Mara lets him.

MARA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
But somebody has to be early.

> FADE OUT.

> THE END <
